  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Paper and Night Sky",
    "overview": "Mamūlāt is built from two materials. By day it is warm, slightly grey paper. After dark it is an olive-black night. A soft sky wash sits at the top of every screen and follows the time of prayer: a blue dawn at Fajr, pale through the day, warmer at Asr, rose-clay at Maghrib, deep night at Isha with a faint drift of stars. The sky fades into the page within the first screenful, so content always sits on plain paper or plain night.\n\nEverything else stays quiet. There are no cards with shadows. Sections sit directly on the page, divided by hairlines, the way lines divide a register. Large regular-weight Prata headlines carry the voice, and Archivo carries the work. One soft clay accent marks what matters: the action, \"done\", the active tab. Gold is kept for the teacher's voice alone.\n\nThe controls are calm but tactile. Buttons have a faint inset fill and shrink slightly under the finger, the main \"Done\" control is a circle you tick, and sheets glide on springs with no bounce. Restraint is the default. Feedback through touch is the one thing the system adds.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Two themes, paper and night, are equal citizens; both are designed, neither is derived.",
      "A prayer-time sky at the top of every screen; flat page below it.",
      "Hairlines instead of boxes; no card shadows.",
      "One clay accent; gold only for the teacher.",
      "Prata display at regular weight, Archivo for UI; IBM Plex Sans Arabic for Arabic/Urdu entries.",
      "44px tap targets, springs without bounce, Undo instead of confirmation."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Clay Rule",
        "body": "Clay is the only accent. If a screen needs a second emphasis, use weight or size, not a new hue.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Gold Belongs to the Teacher Rule",
        "body": "Gold appears only where the teacher is speaking. Never use it for achievements, streaks or decoration.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Dark Text on Clay Rule",
        "body": "Clay fills always carry on-clay (#252c21) text. White on clay fails contrast and breaks the look.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Regular Weight Rule",
        "body": "Prata is only ever set at 400. Hierarchy comes from size, not bold.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The No Shouting Rule",
        "body": "No tracked all-caps labels. Headings are sentence case.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Figures Rule",
        "body": "Every number that can change (percentages, streaks, counts, dates in grids) uses tabular figures (`num`).",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Script Respect Rule",
        "body": "Student-entered text always gets `dir=\"auto\"`. Right-to-left runs switch to IBM Plex Sans Arabic at line-height 1.7 with no letter-spacing.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
